-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  dir_prologue.pcpp - Directive compliance: where build directives stand

  BNF.md sec 1 / 2: build directives stand only in the directive prologue,
  right after module ...; and before the import clause. A single-valued
  directive (@optimize, @subsystem, ...) is given at most once; the list
  directives (@cppdefine, @linklibrary, @add..., @copydll, @cppundef)
  accumulate. In an imported unit the main-module-only directives (@optimize,
  @subsystem, @target, @addverinfo, @unitTestMode, @vi*, @exeicon,
  @addmodulepath) are errors, the list directives add to the one build, and
  @breakpoint / @message are allowed. This exe gives two @cppdefine (both
  reach the emitted C++), and the unit dir_prologue_unit.pcpp it imports adds
  a third.

  How to check, from repo\bin:
    pcpp res\tests\dir_prologue.pcpp -r
    pcpp res\tests\dir_prologue.pcpp -r -t x86_64-linux-gnu   (runs in WSL)
  EXPECT:
    DIR_PRO_A + DIR_PRO_B = 3
    DIR_PRO_FROM_UNIT = 5
    [Heap] Allocs: 0, Frees: 0, Leaked: 0
  EXITCODE: 0
  Both targets print the same; the [Heap] line is the debug build's heap report.

  Break it: run each file below the same way; each reports exactly what the
  // ERROR / WARN / HINT comment on its marked line says, and nothing else:
    dir_prologue_err_unknown.pcpp      a directive PaskalCPP does not have
    dir_prologue_err_shape.pcpp        @optimize with a string, not a word
    dir_prologue_err_place.pcpp        a build directive after a declaration
    dir_prologue_err_twice.pcpp        a single-valued directive given twice
    dir_prologue_err_mainonly.pcpp     @optimize in an imported unit (the report
                                       is in dir_prologue_err_mainonly_unit.pcpp)
    dir_prologue_err_breakpoint.pcpp   @breakpoint in the prologue: a warning,
                                       the build succeeds
*******************************************************************************/

module exe dir_prologue;
@cppdefine "DIR_PRO_A" "1";
@cppdefine "DIR_PRO_B" "2";
import dir_prologue_unit;

begin
  println("DIR_PRO_A + DIR_PRO_B = {}", cpp("DIR_PRO_A + DIR_PRO_B"));
  println("DIR_PRO_FROM_UNIT = {}", cpp("DIR_PRO_FROM_UNIT"));
end.
